---
import ToolShell from '@/components/ToolShell.astro';
import HardenGenerator from '@/components/tools/HardenGenerator.astro';
import { localePath } from '@/consts';
---

<ToolShell
  lang="en"
  slug="harden"
  title="systemd hardening generator"
  description="Tick the hardening you want and get a paste-ready systemd unit file — with the cost of every directive written next to it. Runs entirely in your browser."
>
  <HardenGenerator lang="en" />

  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:2rem">
    <h2>Why the cost matters more than the directive</h2>
    <p>
      Copying a list of hardening directives is easy; knowing which one will break your service at 03:00 is not.
      Every item above says what it restricts <em>and</em> what it takes away — a private <code>/dev</code> means no GPU,
      <code>MemoryDenyWriteExecute</code> means no JIT, <code>RestrictNamespaces</code> means anything that runs
      containers stops working.
    </p>
    <p>
      The workflow that actually works: enable, then run a real workload, then read the journal. A blocked syscall
      with <code>SystemCallErrorNumber=EPERM</code> shows up as a permission error rather than a crash, which is
      exactly what you want when you are bisecting.
    </p>
    <p>
      Related reading: <a href={localePath('en', '/posts/systemd-hardening/')}>systemd hardening</a>,
      <a href={localePath('en', '/posts/systemd-unit-cookbook/')}>the systemd unit cookbook</a>,
      <a href={localePath('en', '/posts/linux-capabilities/')}>Linux capabilities</a>, and
      <a href={localePath('en', '/posts/container-seccomp-hardening/')}>seccomp in containers</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The output above is checked with systemd's own tool. <code>systemd-analyze verify</code> on the default
      and on the all-options-on preset reports exactly one thing — <code>Command /usr/local/bin/&lt;name&gt; is not
      executable</code> — because it is a template and that path is yours to fill in. No unknown or malformed
      directives.
    </p>
  </div>
</ToolShell>
